Embedding and C Interop.Calling Lua From C: the Basics:
This page shows the minimal C code needed to embed a Lua interpreter, run a script, and call a Lua function from C — the "C calls Lua" direction introduced on the previous page.

## Setting up: linking against Lua

Compiling C code that embeds Lua requires linking against the Lua library and including its headers:

```bash
# Arch Linux: the lua package includes headers and the linkable library
gcc my_program.c -o my_program -llua -lm
```

(The exact linker flags can vary slightly by how Lua was packaged on your system — `pkg-config --cflags --libs lua5.4` is a portable way to get the correct flags, if your distro's Lua package provides pkg-config metadata.)

## A minimal embedding example: running a script from C

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lua.h>
#include <lualib.h>
#include <lauxlib.h>

int main(void) {
    lua_State *L = luaL_newstate();   // create a new Lua interpreter state
    luaL_openlibs(L);                   // load the standard library (print, string, table, etc.)

    // run a Lua script from a file
    if (luaL_dofile(L, "script.lua") != LUA_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: %s\n", lua_tostring(L, -1));
        lua_pop(L, 1);
    }

    lua_close(L); // clean up the interpreter state
    return 0;
}
```

```lua
-- script.lua
print("Hello from a Lua script, run by C!")
```

`lua_State*` represents one complete, independent Lua interpreter instance — a host application can even run multiple, isolated Lua states simultaneously if needed (useful for sandboxing different scripts from each other). `luaL_openlibs` loads Lua's standard library into that state; without it, even `print` wouldn't be available, since a freshly created `lua_State` starts genuinely empty, giving the host application full control over what's available.

## Calling a specific Lua function from C

```c
#include <lua.h>
#include <lualib.h>
#include <lauxlib.h>

int main(void) {
    lua_State *L = luaL_newstate();
    luaL_openlibs(L);
    luaL_dofile(L, "script.lua"); // this script defines a function called "add"

    lua_getglobal(L, "add");   // push the Lua function "add" onto the stack
    lua_pushnumber(L, 3);        // push the first argument
    lua_pushnumber(L, 4);          // push the second argument

    if (lua_pcall(L, 2, 1, 0) != LUA_OK) { // call it: 2 args in, 1 result out, no error handler
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: %s\n", lua_tostring(L, -1));
    } else {
        double result = lua_tonumber(L, -1); // read the result off the top of the stack
        printf("Result: %g\n", result);
    }

    lua_close(L);
    return 0;
}
```

```lua
-- script.lua
function add(a, b)
    return a + b
end
```

## Understanding the stack model

Every one of these C API calls manipulates a shared **stack**: `lua_getglobal` pushes the function, `lua_pushnumber` pushes each argument (in order), and `lua_pcall(L, nargs, nresults, msgh)` pops the function and its arguments off the stack, calls it, and pushes the results back on — `lua_tonumber(L, -1)` then reads the topmost stack value (`-1` meaning "one from the top," following the same negative-indexing convention as Lua's own `string.sub` from [Strings](/lua/strings/)). This stack-based protocol is consistent across the entire C API — once it clicks for one function, the pattern generalizes to essentially every other C API call.

