Embedding and C Interop.Calling C From Lua:
The reverse direction: exposing a C function so Lua scripts can call it directly — this is how a host application (a game engine, for example) gives scripts access to its own functionality.

## Writing a C function Lua can call

Every C function exposed to Lua must follow a specific signature: it takes one `lua_State*` argument and returns an `int` (the number of return values it's pushing onto the stack):

```c
#include <lua.h>
#include <lualib.h>
#include <lauxlib.h>

// a C function callable from Lua: multiplies two numbers
static int l_multiply(lua_State *L) {
    double a = luaL_checknumber(L, 1); // get argument 1, error if it's not a number
    double b = luaL_checknumber(L, 2); // get argument 2

    lua_pushnumber(L, a * b); // push the result

    return 1; // we're returning ONE value
}
```

`luaL_checknumber(L, 1)` reads the first argument the Lua script passed, and — importantly — automatically raises a proper Lua error (rather than crashing or producing garbage) if the caller passed something that isn't a number, giving Lua scripts a predictable, safe error experience even when misused.

## Registering the function and making it available to Lua

```c
int main(void) {
    lua_State *L = luaL_newstate();
    luaL_openlibs(L);

    lua_pushcfunction(L, l_multiply); // push our C function onto the stack
    lua_setglobal(L, "multiply");        // and bind it to the global name "multiply"

    luaL_dofile(L, "script.lua"); // this script can now call multiply(...) directly

    lua_close(L);
    return 0;
}
```

```lua
-- script.lua
local result = multiply(6, 7)
print(result) -- 42
```

From the Lua script's perspective, `multiply` is now indistinguishable from any function defined in pure Lua — it can be called, passed around, stored in a table, exactly like any other value, even though its actual implementation lives entirely in C.

## Exposing a whole table of related functions (a "module," from C)

```c
static const luaL_Reg mymodule_functions[] = {
    {"multiply", l_multiply},
    {"add", l_add}, // (implementation omitted for brevity, same pattern as l_multiply)
    {NULL, NULL} // sentinel, marks the end of the array
};

int main(void) {
    lua_State *L = luaL_newstate();
    luaL_openlibs(L);

    luaL_newlib(L, mymodule_functions); // creates a new table, populated with the functions
    lua_setglobal(L, "mymodule");          // bind it to the global name "mymodule"

    luaL_dofile(L, "script.lua");

    lua_close(L);
    return 0;
}
```

```lua
-- script.lua
print(mymodule.multiply(3, 4)) -- 12
print(mymodule.add(3, 4))         -- 7
```

This is exactly how real embedded-Lua applications expose their functionality — a game engine might expose a `game` table with functions like `game.spawn_entity`, `game.play_sound`, `game.get_player_position`, each implemented in C but called naturally from Lua scripts, using precisely this registration pattern.

## Why this matters for you as a Lua scripter

Even if you never write this C code yourself, understanding this mechanism explains something important about scripting in an embedded context: **any function available to your Lua script (beyond the standard library) was deliberately exposed by the host application** — you cannot call arbitrary C functions or access memory directly from Lua; you're limited to exactly whatever API surface the host chose to register. This is precisely the sandboxing benefit mentioned in [Why Embed Lua](/lua/why-embed-lua/) — it's a deliberate safety property of the embedding architecture, not a limitation of the Lua language itself.

